---
title: Lasagna
description: "Lasagna made entirely from scratch: green spinach pasta, a slow-cooked beef and pork ragù and béchamel."
author: Andy Cooks
source: https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-__qVqib9Pw
course: main
cuisine: italian
diet: meat
difficulty: hard
servings: 8
time: 6h
prep time: 1h
cook time: 5h
tags: [pasta, beef, pork, baked, make-ahead]
---

Dice the @carrot{2%medium}, @onion{1%medium}, and @celery stalk{2} into a fine brunoise (very small dice). Mince the @garlic{4%cloves}. Prepare a @Parmesan rind{1%piece} to add later for umami.

In a #Dutch oven{} over high heat, add @olive oil{2%tbsp}. \
Once smoking, break up the @beef mince{500%g}(80/20) into small pieces and add in batches so it browns rather than stews. \
Season with @salt{to taste} and sear for ~beef_sear{8%minutes} to build a good crust and deep meat flavor (Maillard reaction). \
Remove and set aside. Repeat with the remaining beef and the @high-fat pork mince{500%g}.

Drain the excess fat from the pot, leaving about @rendered fat{1%tbsp} in the pan. \
Add the carrots, season with salt, and cook for a couple of minutes. \
Add the onions and sweat for ~onions_sweat{2%minutes}, then add the celery and garlic. \
Sweating the veggies develops their natural sugars, which balances the ragu and eliminates the need to add sugar later!

Return the seared meat to the pot. Pour in @full-bodied red wine{250%ml}(like Sangiovese or Shiraz). \
Reduce for ~wine_reduction{5%minutes} to cook off the harsh alcohol.

Pour in @tomato passata{700%ml}. Rinse the passata jar with a little @water{100%ml} and add that to the pot as well. \
Submerge the Parmesan rind into the sauce. \
Bring to a very slow simmer and let cook for ideally ~ragu_simmer{4%hours} to ~ragu_simmer{6%hours} (stirring every 30 mins). \
If it gets too dry, add a splash of water.

While the ragu cooks, make the green pasta dough. Boil a #large pot{} of salted water. \
Blanch @baby spinach{100%g} until just wilted, then plunge into an ice bath to keep it bright green. \
Squeeze out as much liquid as possible. \
Blend the spinach with @eggs{3} in a #blender{} until completely smooth (start on low speed so you don't cook the eggs, then increase to high).

Pour @Type 00 flour{320%g} onto a clean bench and create a well in the center. \
Pour the spinach-egg mixture and @olive oil{1%tbsp} into the well. \
Gradually incorporate the flour using a fork, then chop it in with a dough scraper. \
Knead with your hands for ~kneading{5%minutes} until the dough is smooth. \
Wrap the dough tightly in cling film and let it rest in the fridge for at least ~dough_rest{1%hour}.

Roll out the pasta dough using a #pasta machine{}. \
Fold the dough in thirds (envelope fold) and pass it through the thickest setting a few times to laminate it. \
Gradually reduce the thickness setting until you reach the second-to-thinnest setting. \
Cut the sheets so they will fit exactly into your #baking dish{}.

Boil strongly salted water in a large pot. \
Cook the pasta sheets one at a time for ~blanching{45%seconds}. \
Immediately plunge each sheet into an ice-water bath to stop the cooking, then drain and lay flat on a tray.

Once the ragu has simmered and is thick and rich, remove the Parmesan rind. \
Stir in @milk{100%ml} to make the sauce extra luscious. \
Cool the ragu completely before assembling (spreading it on a flat tray in the fridge helps speed this up). Cold ragu prevents the fat from rendering out during assembly and makes it much easier to layer evenly.

To make the bechamel, melt @butter{50%g} in a #saucepan{} over medium heat. \
Whisk in @all-purpose flour{50%g} and a pinch of salt to create a blonde roux. Cook for a few minutes until it smells like cookies.

Gradually whisk in @milk{600%ml}, adding about 100ml at a time. \
Ensure all lumps are completely dissolved before adding the next splash of milk. \
Cook over medium heat until the sauce thickens enough to coat the back of a spoon. \
Remove from heat and season with salt and freshly grated @nutmeg{1/8%whole}.

Assemble the lasagna (aiming for 5 layers of pasta). \
Spread a 1/5th portion of the cooled meat sauce evenly on the bottom of the baking dish. \
Cover with a layer of pasta (trim to fit so there is no excessive overlap). \
Spread a layer of bechamel, then sprinkle with grated @mozzarella cheese{60%g} and grated @Parmesan cheese{20%g}. \
Repeat this process until you have 4 meat layers done.

For the final (5th) layer, place your nicest sheet of pasta on top. \
Instead of meat sauce, spread a thin layer of @tomato passata{100%ml} on the top sheet to prevent a greasy finish and keep the pasta from drying out.

Cover the baking dish tightly with aluminum foil. \
Bake in an oven preheated to 180 °C for ~bake_covered{40%minutes}.

Remove the foil carefully. \
Add a layer of fresh @basil leaves{1%handful}, followed by the remaining @mozzarella cheese{60%g} and @Parmesan cheese{20%g}. \
Return to the oven and bake uncovered for ~bake_uncovered{15%minutes} until bubbly and a lovely golden brown. (Use the grill/broiler function for the last few minutes if needed).

Let the lasagna rest for at least ~rest_lasagna{10%minutes} before slicing so it holds its shape. Enjoy!
